## 🎤 9. Interview Preparation

Try answering each question before expanding the answer.

<details>
<summary><strong>1. What is DevOps?</strong></summary>

DevOps combines practices that help development and operations teams build, release, and run software reliably. It emphasises collaboration, automation, and feedback.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>2. Is DevOps a tool?</strong></summary>

No. Tools support DevOps, but DevOps also involves shared responsibility, team practices, and repeatable processes.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>3. What is continuous integration?</strong></summary>

CI is the practice of integrating small changes frequently and checking them through automated builds and tests.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>4. How do continuous delivery and deployment differ?</strong></summary>

Continuous delivery keeps validated changes ready to release, potentially with a human production-release decision. Continuous deployment automatically releases qualifying changes after the required checks pass.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>5. What is a build artifact?</strong></summary>

A build artifact is an output that can be stored, tested, or deployed, such as a React build folder, Java JAR, or container image.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>6. How are Git and GitHub different?</strong></summary>

Git is a distributed version control system. GitHub hosts Git repositories and provides collaboration features.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>7. What should happen when a required CI test fails?</strong></summary>

The pipeline should report the failure and block promotion. Investigate the cause, fix it, and rerun the checks.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>8. Why might an application work locally but fail on a server?</strong></summary>

The environments may differ in dependency versions, configuration, permissions, or network access. Start with logs and compare the environments systematically.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>9. How are Vagrant and a virtualisation provider different?</strong></summary>

Vagrant manages VM environments through configuration and commands. The provider supplies the underlying capability to run the VMs.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>10. Does a successful pipeline mean there are no bugs?</strong></summary>

No. It means the configured checks passed. Missing tests or differences in real-world conditions can still expose problems.

</details>

